courses.learning_outcomes:
Primary Learning Outcomes:

By the end of this course, students will be able to:
1. express ideas orally using clear and comprehensible language;
2. identify and interpret a text's main message and attitude;
3. understand, evaluate and respond critically to information in persuasive texts;
4. identify common features of academic texts;
5. understand and implement a process-based approach to writing tasks;
6. write a coherent expository text showing a degree of grammatical accuracy, lexical range, and syntactic variety and complexity; and
7. avoid plagiarism by using appropriate writing strategies.

Secondary Learning Outcomes:
By the end of this course, students will be able to:
1. reflect on their own language learning
2. utilize independent learning strategies

Course Objective

To enhance students’ listening and speaking competence by:

(1) appreciating and attending to some essential features of the listening process;
(2) appreciating and attending to some essential features of spoken English;
(3) identifying and employing appropriate strategies for effective communication;
(4) engaging in simulated/authentic language learning/language use situations;
(5) evaluating for themselves their listening and speaking processes.

Performance Objectives

By the end of the course, students should be able to listen and respond effectively by:

(1) processing the linguistic forms;
(2) identifying the intention of the speaker(s);
(3) understanding the whole message rather than individual utterances;
(4) interpreting and evaluating the message conveyed;
(5) responding appropriately in various settings.

By the end of the course, students should be able to listen and respond effectively by:

1. showing more competency in processing linguistic forms for meaning;
2. identifying the intention of the writer and the speaker;
3. understanding messages received and be able to respond appropriately;
4. developing reading aloud skills which are used in classroom presentation;
5. preparing and delivering an oral presentation and managing the subsequent discussion;
6. participating in group / panel discussions;
7. reflecting on learning progress and identifying areas for further development.

By the end of this course, students will have further developed their skills in:

1. Understand the purpose of academic writing;
2. Establish a valid research/essay topic;
3. Understand the important stages of the writing process;
4. Paraphrase and summarize the reading material;
5. Blend quotes and paraphrases into the text;
6. Organize a cohesive and coherent text;
7. Employ vocabulary and sentence structures at an appropriate level of formality;
8. Use the APA format to document sources properly; and
9. Engage in self-initiated independent language learning through online resources.

By the end of this course, students will be able to:

1. select appropriate academic sources;
2. summarise academic texts;
3. evaluate academic texts;
4. organise and compile an annotated bibliography;
5. develop a coherent essay plan;
6. write an essay that advances a clear and well-supported argument using appropriate academic register and language; and

7. incorporate sources effectively into their work.

By the end of this course, students will be able to:

- understand the important features of academic persuasive essays through reading texts;
- apply effective reading strategies to enhance understanding of academic texts;
- paraphrase, summarize, and quote reading materials properly;
- develop a logical and coherent argument; and
- deliver a well-organized presentation in an academic context.
